WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122
A useful way to read requirements is to look for different kinds of language.

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:07.362
Nouns often suggest candidate entities.

00:00:07.362 --> 00:00:10.247
Descriptive facts often become attributes.

00:00:10.247 --> 00:00:13.107
Verbs often reveal relationships.

00:00:13.107 --> 00:00:19.517
Words such as must, may, only, and at least often reveal constraints or optionality.

00:00:19.517 --> 00:00:23.167
For example, patient and provider are nouns.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:26.492
Birth date is a descriptive fact about a patient.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:30.157
A patient books an appointment describes a relationship.

00:00:30.157 --> 00:00:35.227
Every appointment must have one provider describes a required relationship.

00:00:35.227 --> 00:00:38.217
Do not start by typing CREATE TABLE.

00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:41.482
Start by writing the business meaning in plain language.

00:00:41.482 --> 00:00:47.012
Once the meaning is clear, translating it into a relational structure becomes much easier.

00:00:47.012 --> 00:00:53.077
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:53.077 --> 00:00:58.392
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:05.722
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:11.562
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:17.147
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
